**Question:** why is `this.name` undefined inside the `forEach` callback, and how do you fix it without adding any variables?

## The setup

```javascript
const team = {
  name: 'Platform',
  members: ['Ada', 'Bo', 'Cy'],

  list() {
    this.members.forEach(function (member) {
      console.log(`${member} is on ${this.name}`);   // this.name is undefined
    });
  },
};

team.list();
// Ada is on undefined
// Bo is on undefined
// Cy is on undefined
```

## Why

`this` in JavaScript is set by **how a function is called**, not where it's written.

`team.list()` is a method call, so inside `list`, `this` is `team` — that part works. But the callback passed to `forEach` is invoked **by `forEach` itself**, as a plain function call with no receiver. There's no `team.` in front of it.

For a plain call, `this` is `undefined` in strict mode (including all module and class code) or the global object in sloppy mode. Either way it isn't `team`, so `this.name` is `undefined` — or throws outright in strict mode.

`this.members` works and `this.name` doesn't, which is what makes this confusing: they're in different functions.

## Fix 1: an arrow function

```javascript
list() {
  this.members.forEach((member) => {
    console.log(`${member} is on ${this.name}`);
  });
}
```

Arrow functions have **no `this` of their own**. They resolve it lexically, from the enclosing scope — which is `list`, where `this` is `team`. Nothing is added, nothing is bound; the arrow simply never creates the binding that was shadowing it.

This is the answer to give.

## Fix 2: `forEach`'s second argument

Less known, and a good one to mention:

```javascript
list() {
  this.members.forEach(function (member) {
    console.log(`${member} is on ${this.name}`);
  }, this);          // ← thisArg
}
```

`forEach` accepts a **`thisArg`** as its second parameter and calls the callback with that as `this`. `map`, `filter`, `some`, `every`, `find`, and `flatMap` all take it too.

Note it has no effect on an arrow function — arrows ignore any attempt to set `this`, including `call`, `apply`, and `bind`.

## Fix 3: `.bind(this)`

```javascript
this.members.forEach(function (member) {
  console.log(`${member} is on ${this.name}`);
}.bind(this));
```

Works, but allocates a new bound function on every call. Fine to know, rarely the best choice.

## The fix that's disqualified

The classic pre-ES6 workaround introduces a variable, which the question rules out:

```javascript
const self = this;                        // not allowed here
this.members.forEach(function (member) {
  console.log(`${member} is on ${self.name}`);
});
```

Worth naming as the historical answer, then giving the arrow.

## The general rule

| Call form | `this` |
|---|---|
| `obj.method()` | `obj` |
| `fn()` | `undefined` (strict) / `globalThis` (sloppy) |
| `new Fn()` | the new instance |
| `fn.call(x)` / `.apply(x)` / `.bind(x)` | `x` |
| arrow function | inherited lexically — cannot be changed |
| callback passed to a library | whatever that library calls it with |

